<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lateen-sail">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lateen sail</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Lateen, Latin rig</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A lateen is a triangular sail set on a long yard mounted at an angle on the mast and running fore and aft; it originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> as early as the 2nd century CE, in Roman times. It became common there by the 5th century and by the 6th had largely replaced the square sail, a change suggested to have been made on cost grounds, as the two rigs performed much alike. Arab seafarers adopted it after the Muslim conquests, and it later rigged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">caravels</a> and the mizzens of full-rigged ships.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lateen-sail.jpg" alt="Lateen sail" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister der Predigten des Heiligen Gregor von Nazianz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meister_der_Predigten_des_Heiligen_Gregor_von_Nazianz_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The lateen developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> as early as the 2nd century CE, in Roman times, and was common there by the 5th century. A related form, the settee, has a short vertical luff, so that it looks like a triangular lateen with the front corner cut off. A 2nd-century gravestone depicts a quadrilateral sail of this kind, and a 4th-century mosaic shows a triangular one, which became the standard rig of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><p>The earliest excavated ship reconstructed with a lateen rig, Yassi Ada II, dates to about 400 CE, and four more are attested before the Arab advance into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The Kelenderis ship mosaic of the late 5th or early 6th century and the Kellia ship graffito of the early 7th century add to the picture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>By the 6th century the lateen had largely replaced the square sail throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. It became the standard rig of the Byzantine dromon war galley and was probably used by the flagship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> in the invasion of the Vandal Kingdom in 532. It is a widespread misconception that it won out through better windward performance. A study of the two rigs found their performance very similar, and the change in the 5th century is suggested to have been made on cost grounds, since the lateen had fewer components and less cordage to replace.</p><p>After the Muslim conquests the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> adopted the lateen through the Coptic population, which shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> maritime tradition and supplied most galley crews for Muslim fleets. From the Mediterranean the sail spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, where the felucca was developed. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> the square rig predominated until about 1500, after which nearly all vessels became lateen rigged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Until the 14th century the lateen was used chiefly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, while Atlantic and Baltic vessels relied on square sails. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">Caravels</a> typically carried three or more lateens. Because the long yard was difficult and dangerous to handle on larger ships in storms, the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrack">carrack</a> confined it to the mizzen mast, and in the early 19th century European ships replaced it with the driver or spanker.</p><p>Small craft kept the rig. The Baltic used it until the late 19th century, and the gundalows north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> carried it into the early twentieth. The lateen was also the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a> rig, by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> bezaan rig, introduced to Bermuda early in the 17th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lateen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>2nd century CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>The Mediterranean</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>An older view gave it an eastern origin, in Arab seafaring.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sail">Sail<span>6th millennium BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">Caravel<span>c. 1255</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=lateen-sail">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
